second vertical OOTD take, not a montage and not a sequence of clips. <Subject 1> performs one uninterrupted flowing fashion-dance movement in the same room while the clothing alone changes from <Subject 2> through <Subject 8> in that exact order. All six clothing transformations happen inside the same continuous body movement. There are no cuts, no scene changes, no time jumps, no pose resets, and no camera changes.\n\nretention_analysis:\n<Subject 1> (appears continuously from 00:00.000-00:15.000 in the only shot): fully_preserved - preserve identity, face, hair, body proportions, hands, body position, continuous motion trajectory, background, lighting, locked camera, and framing without drift.\n<Subject 2> (worn from 00:00.000-00:02.000): attribute_transfer - transfer Outfit 1 from <Picture 2> onto <Subject 1>; do not transfer any clothing model, face, body, pose, or background from the garment reference.\n<Subject 3> (worn from 00:02.000-00:04.100): attribute_transfer - transfer Outfit 2 from <Picture 3> onto the same continuously moving <Subject 1> only.\n<Subject 4> (worn from 00:04.100-00:06.200): attribute_transfer - transfer Outfit 3 from <Picture 4> onto the same continuously moving <Subject 1> only.\n<Subject 5> (worn from 00:06.200-00:08.300): attribute_transfer - transfer Outfit 4 from <Picture 5> onto the same continuously moving <Subject 1> only.\n<Subject 6> (worn from 00:08.300-00:10.400): attribute_transfer - transfer Outfit 5 from <Picture 6> onto the same continuously moving <Subject 1> only.\n<Subject 7> (worn from 00:10.400-00:12.500): attribute_transfer - transfer Outfit 6 from <Picture 7> onto the same continuously moving <Subject 1> only.\n<Subject 8> (worn from 00:12.500-00:15.000): attribute_transfer - transfer Outfit 7 from <Picture 8> onto the same continuously moving <Subject 1> only.\n\ndetailed_description:\nThe target is a polished realistic vertical OOTD try-on video created as one continuous 15-second take. Use a locked camera and fixed focal length. Preserve the portrait framing from <Picture 1>, showing the model from the top of her head to below the skirt or upper calves; her feet remain outside the frame.\n\nThe model behaves like a real fashion creator presenting dresses, not like a dancer. Her posture is relaxed and elegant. Her elbows remain close to her body, her wrists stay soft, and her gestures remain within the area between her hair, collarbone, waist, and skirt. She uses gentle weight shifts, subtle hip movement, small head tilts, light hair-touching gestures, and restrained quarter-turns to display each outfit naturally.\n\nEvery gesture flows into the next one, but she is not constantly waving her arms. After each small movement, allow a brief natural settling beat with breathing and subtle body motion. Do not freeze, restart, or return to an identical neutral pose.\n\n[Shot 1 - the only shot, 00:00.000-00:15.000] <Subject 1> begins wearing <Subject 2>, Outfit 1. She faces the camera with her hands loosely overlapped near her lower waist. Her weight rests softly on one hip. She gives a slight head tilt and a relaxed smile while her upper body makes a very small natural sway.\n\nAt 00:02.000, as her hands separate gently and one hand begins rising toward her hair, only the clothing transforms into <Subject 3>, Outfit 2. Her raised hand continues along the same path and lightly tucks a loose strand of hair beside her ear. Her other hand remains relaxed near her waist. She lowers her chin briefly, then looks back toward the camera.\n\nHer hair-touching gesture flows into a restrained quarter-turn toward her left. One hand rests lightly at the waist while the other hangs naturally beside the skirt. At 00:04.100, during this continuing quarter-turn, only the clothing transforms into <Subject 4>, Outfit 3. She completes the small turn, showing the dress silhouette without turning her back or spinning.\n\nShe smoothly returns toward the camera. Her fingertips lightly follow the waistline and then descend toward the side of the skirt. At 00:06.200, while her hand continues down the side seam, only the clothing transforms into <Subject 5>, Outfit 4. She briefly lowers her gaze toward the outfit, then raises her eyes toward the camera with a soft smile.\n\nHer weight shifts gently toward the opposite hip. One hand remains near the waist while the other rises naturally toward the collarbone. At 00:08.300, during this small weight shift, only the clothing transforms into <Subject 6>, Outfit 5. Her shoulders make a subtle counter-tilt while her hands remain close to her body.\n\nThe hand near her collarbone lowers gradually. Both hands meet loosely near the lower waist for a moment, then one hand begins tracing the opposite side of the dress. At 00:10.400, during this continuous waist-level gesture, only the clothing transforms into <Subject 7>, Outfit 6.\n\nShe follows the hand movement into a restrained quarter-turn toward the opposite side. Her hips and shoulders rotate only slightly, just enough to display the waist and skirt silhouette. At 00:12.500, during the continuing quarter-turn, only the clothing transforms into <Subject 8>, Outfit 7.\n\nShe returns smoothly to a mostly front-facing position. One hand rises briefly to the side of her neck or hair while the other remains relaxed near her hip. She finishes with one final gentle weight shift, a slight head tilt, direct eye contact, and a warm natural smile at 00:15.000.\n\nAt every clothing transformation, preserve the exact body location, joint positions, hand trajectory, gaze direction, balance, movement direction, and movement speed from the preceding frame. Only the clothing changes.\n\nNo broad arm swings, arm circles, forceful shoulder rolls, repeated hand waving, pumping gestures, energetic dancing, spins, walking, exaggerated hip movement, large torso bends, pose resets, freezes, teleportation, camera movement, flash transitions, or scene cuts.\n\noverall_soundscape:\nQuiet indoor roo